llvm-4.0.0 backport patch: an 8- or 16-bit compare is done at i32 (PS4)

This patch CHANGES CODE GENERATION for an integer comparison of 8 or
16 bits against a value other than zero. Upstream 4.0.0 (since 3.9)
compares at the operand's width (`cmp byte [mem], 1`) and promotes only
a 16-bit compare with an immediate; LLVM 3.5 to 3.8 extended both
operands to i32 first (X86ISelLowering.cpp, EmitCmp: "Do the comparison
at i32 if it's smaller, besides the Atom case"), so the byte is loaded
with movzx and compared as a dword (`movzx eax, byte [mem]; cmp eax,
1`). The game region holds 12 byte and 3 word compares against a
nonzero operand in 21 MB of code (2,205 `cmp byte [mem], 0` and 68
`cmp word [mem], 0`, which go through EmitTest and are not promoted);
the library region, built by 3.3 before the rule, holds 611 and 289.
The masked i1 loads of the region tested as conditions are all
`movzx eax, byte [rip+x]; and eax, 1; cmp eax, 1` (23 sites), which
this rule and the masked-load patch give together; 0xb100c0's flag test
compiles to the base's bytes with both.

